Abstract

This thesis establishes a controlled research program determining the conditions under which a local quantum-information metric — obtained directly from microscopic quantum states, without assuming an AdS/CFT bulk metric — can define a global geometry whose extremal-surface functional reproduces entanglement entropy. The central question is not whether quantum information generates an area law, but: what are the integrability conditions under which a microscopic quantum Fisher information metric (QFIM) defines a global geometry whose extremal-surface functional reproduces entanglement entropy?

We rigorously distinguish four epistemic statuses: THEOREM, CONJECTURE, NUMERICAL EVIDENCE, and HEURISTIC. We formulate the QFIM–Area Integrability Conjecture and identify the fundamental mathematical problem: the characterization of the image of the non-linear minimal-surface operator \mathfrak{R}. We propose a numerical benchmark with three models and five independent residuals designed to falsify the conjecture. We show that the existing literature establishes metric reconstruction from entropy in specific holographic frameworks but does not establish the inverse link starting from a microscopic QFIM. We explicitly distinguish our program from Yaman (2026) and Lee (2026), which construct geometry from QFIM, in that we isolate the integrability condition S \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R} \circ \Phi_{L*}).

