Chapter 1 — Introduction and Motivation

1.1 The Problem of Quantum Gravity

Physics at the fundamental level is described by two extraordinarily successful but mutually incompatible frameworks: Quantum Field Theory (QFT), which describes matter and non-gravitational interactions on a fixed classical spacetime, and General Relativity (GR), which describes gravity as the dynamical curvature of a spacetime that is not a fixed background. The incompatibility is not merely technical. QFT presupposes a classical, smooth spacetime that GR says is dynamical and curved. The central unresolved question is: what is the fundamental ontology of spacetime?

1.2 The Information-Theoretic Turn

A growing body of work suggests that spacetime is not fundamental but emergent from quantum information. Key developments include the Bekenstein–Hawking entropy S_{BH} = A/4G\hbar, the Ryu–Takayanagi formula S_A = \operatorname{Area}(\gamma_A)/4G_N\hbar — established within semiclassical AdS/CFT for static geometries — the ER=EPR conjecture, cMERA and tensor networks, and recent QFIM-based approaches. Yaman (2026) equips an observer's attention parameter space with the QFIM and derives emergent Riemannian geometry bridged to Lorentzian signature via Wick rotation, the entanglement area law from exponential correlation decay, and Newton's constant as an emergent parameter. This work is posted on Zenodo (March 2026) and has not undergone conventional peer review. Lee (2026) postulates that spacetime distance measures informational distinguishability and identifies the emergent metric with the QFIM of the observer's reduced density matrix, demonstrating that the thermodynamic evolution of this informational geometry obeys the Einstein field equations. This work is posted on viXra (January 2026) and has not undergone conventional peer review.

1.3 The Gap

Despite this progress, a fundamental gap remains. The existing literature establishes: (i) the first law of entanglement \delta S = \delta \langle K \rangle is equivalent to the linearized Einstein equations in asymptotically AdS space for spherical regions (Faulkner et al., 2014); (ii) in Gaussian cMERA for a free scalar theory, the entanglement entropy of half-space is an integral along the renormalization scale whose measure is the Fisher information metric of the cMERA circuit (Fernandez-Melgarejo & Molina-Vilaplana, 2021); (iii) the timelike first law of entanglement is equivalent to linearized Einstein equations in asymptotically AdS spacetime (Li, Xiao, He & Sun, JHEP 2026, 144). What is not established is a general theorem specifying sufficient microscopic conditions under which a local quantum-information metric induces a global entanglement area law compatible with holographic gravitational dynamics.

1.4 The Central Question

This thesis addresses the following question: Under what integrability conditions does a quantum Fisher information metric obtained directly from microscopic quantum states define a global geometry whose extremal-surface functional reproduces entanglement entropy?

The problem is not "QFIM \rightarrow area law" but rather:

\boxed{\text{QFIM} + \text{microscopic conditions} \Longrightarrow \text{Area law compatible with } g_Q}

The distinction is fundamental: the QFIM can characterize an information structure without that structure being the emergent physical geometry.

1.5 The Hierarchy of Geometric Claims

We distinguish four levels of increasing strength:

\boxed{G_0 \rightarrow G_1 \rightarrow G_2 \rightarrow G_3}

where G_0: entropy has an area-like scaling; G_1: there exists some metric h reproducing the entropy; G_2: the same h reproduces a sufficiently rich family of regions; G_3: that h is independently obtained from the microscopic QFIM. The scientific target is the bridge G_2 \rightarrow G_3.

