Chapter 2 — Epistemic Framework and Methodology

2.1 The Four Epistemic Statuses

Throughout this thesis, we rigorously distinguish:

· THEOREM: A mathematically proven statement, with a complete proof or a direct reference to a published proof.
· CONJECTURE: A precise mathematical statement whose truth is not established but which is falsifiable.
· NUMERICAL EVIDENCE: A result obtained by simulation or numerical computation, valid within the range of parameters tested, but not a proof.
· HEURISTIC: A plausible but non-rigorous argument, useful for motivation but not for establishing truth.

2.2 The Rule of Non-Circularity

A central methodological principle: do not assume the conclusion. In particular: do not postulate the area law as an axiom; do not postulate the Ryu–Takayanagi formula as an axiom; do not postulate the QFIM as the spacetime metric; do not assume an AdS/CFT bulk metric beforehand.

2.3 The Rule of Defensibility

We replace claims of the form "no one has done it" with the more defensible: the literature surveyed so far does not establish a general theorem specifying sufficient microscopic conditions under which a local quantum-information metric induces a global entanglement area law compatible with holographic gravitational dynamics. Absence of proof found \neq proof of absence in all literature.

2.4 The Train/Test Methodology

This thesis introduces a methodological innovation: a train/test split in the physics. Let \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{C}_{\text{train}} \cup \mathcal{C}_{\text{test}}. Use \mathcal{C}_{\text{train}} to determine \eta and any permitted reconstruction parameters. Then evaluate:

\Delta_A^{\text{test}} = \frac{\|S - \eta \mathfrak{R}_{h_Q}\|_{\mathcal{C}_{\text{test}}}}{\|S\|_{\mathcal{C}_{\text{test}}} + \epsilon}

If the QFIM-derived geometry genuinely explains the entropy functional, it should generalize to regions that were not used in reconstruction. This turns the thesis from a fitting exercise into a genuine falsification experiment.

