Chapter 5 — Area Laws: What Is Established

5.1 The Area Law in 1D

THEOREM (Hastings, 2007). For the ground state of a gapped local Hamiltonian in 1D with gap \Delta and interaction strength J, the entanglement entropy of a region A satisfies S_A \leq C \cdot |\partial A| where C depends only on \Delta and J.

THEOREM (Brandão–Horodecki, 2013). For 1D quantum states, exponential decay of correlations implies an area law for the entanglement entropy. The state can be approximated by an MPS with polynomial bond dimension. CORRECTION from v2.0: This is not an equivalence between MPS and finite correlation length. Injective MPS have finite correlation length; conversely, finite correlation length states can be approximated by MPS.

5.2 The Area Law in Higher Dimensions

NUMERICAL EVIDENCE: For gapped local Hamiltonians in d > 1, the area law is observed numerically but not proven in general. THEOREM (free fermions): For free fermionic systems, the area law holds with logarithmic corrections at criticality.

5.3 Violations of the Area Law

THEOREM (Calabrese–Cardy, 2004). At criticality in 1D, S_A \sim (c/3) \log L where c is the central charge.

THEOREM (Gong et al., 2017). For 1/r^\alpha long-range interactions in D dimensions, the entanglement entropy cannot change faster than a rate proportional to the boundary area for any \alpha > D + 1. For \alpha > 2D + 2, the ground state satisfies the area law if it can be transformed along a gapped adiabatic path into a state known to satisfy the area law.

THEOREM (Kuwahara–Saito, 2020). For generic non-critical one-dimensional ground states with locally bounded Hamiltonians, the area law robustly holds without corrections, even under long-range interactions.

CORRECTION from v2.0: The statement "non-locality implies area-law violation" is false. Long-range interactions do not automatically destroy the area law.

5.4 The Ryu–Takayanagi Formula

ESTABLISHED HOLOGRAPHIC PRESCRIPTION (Ryu–Takayanagi, 2006; Lewkowycz–Maldacena, 2013). In the AdS/CFT correspondence, the entanglement entropy of a boundary region A is given by S_A = \operatorname{Area}(\gamma_A)/4G_N \hbar where \gamma_A is the minimal surface in the bulk homologous to A. This is established within the semiclassical AdS/CFT framework. It is not a mathematical theorem for arbitrary quantum systems.

