Chapter 6 — The Integrability Problem

6.1 The Fundamental Problem

We do not ask "does QFIM generate an area law?" We ask: what are the integrability conditions under which a microscopic QFIM defines a global geometry whose extremal-surface functional reproduces entropy?

6.2 The Forward Functional

DEFINITION 6.1 (Minimal-Surface Functional). Define the forward functional:

\mathfrak{R}: \mathcal{M}_{\text{adm}}/\operatorname{Diff}(M) \rightarrow \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{C})

by

\mathfrak{R}[h](A) = \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A[h])

where \gamma_A[h] is the h-minimal codimension-one surface satisfying \partial \gamma_A = \partial A and H_h[\gamma_A] = 0. Then the inverse problem becomes:

\boxed{S/\eta \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R})}

and the QFIM restriction becomes:

\boxed{S/\eta \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R} \circ \Phi_{L*})}

6.3 Three Levels of Geometric Integrability

DEFINITION 6.2 (Geometric Integrability — E_1). S \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R}) if there exists a metric h such that S_A = \eta \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A) for all regions A in a sufficiently rich class.

DEFINITION 6.3 (Identifiability — E_2). h_S := \arg\min_h \Delta_A[h] is identifiable from S if the map S \mapsto h_S is unique up to isometry within the class of metrics considered.

DEFINITION 6.4 (Full Information-Geometric Realizability — E_3). h_S = h_Q := \Phi_{L*} g_Q, i.e., the metric reconstructed from S coincides with the metric induced from the QFIM via the coarse-graining map.

This three-level structure clarifies that E_1 is necessary but not sufficient for E_2, which is necessary but not sufficient for E_3.

6.4 The Image Problem

The most precise formulation:

\boxed{S_A / \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R}_{g_Q}) \quad ?}

where \mathfrak{R}_{g_Q} is the non-linear minimal-surface operator. This transforms the question into a testable inverse problem.

