Chapter 8 — The Hessian–Jacobi Correspondence

8.1 First Variation Compatibility

PROPOSITION 8.1 (First Variation Compatibility). If S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A), then a variation of region A must produce the corresponding geometric variation:

\delta S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \delta \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A)

For an on-shell minimal surface whose boundary is being varied, the relevant variation reduces to a boundary term, not generically an integral over the entire minimal surface:

\boxed{
\delta \mathfrak{R}_h[A] = \int_{\partial \gamma_A} \Pi_i \, \delta X^i
}

up to the precise convention for the canonical momentum and boundary measure. Then:

\boxed{
\delta S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \, \delta \mathfrak{R}_h[A]
}

8.2 Second Variation Compatibility (Corrected)

CORRECTION from v2.0. The previous residual \Delta_2 = \|\delta^2 S - \eta J_{g_Q}^{-1}\| was mathematically problematic. The Jacobi operator J_h is a differential operator on normal deformations. The second variation of area is directly associated with J_h:

\delta^2 \mathcal{A}_h[\phi, \psi] = \int_{\gamma_A} \phi \, J_h \, \psi \, d\mu_\gamma

where:

\boxed{J_h = -\Delta_\gamma - \left( |K|^2 + \operatorname{Ric}_h(n, n) \right)}

with K the extrinsic curvature tensor and \operatorname{Ric}_h(n,n) the Ricci curvature in the normal direction.

DOMAIN MISMATCH CORRECTION. But K_S is an entropic Hessian with respect to boundary-region deformation, whereas J_h acts on normal deformations of the bulk minimal surface. These are not automatically the same vector space. The correct structure is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{H}_S = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \, \mathcal{B}_h^{\,*} J_h \mathcal{B}_h
}

where \mathcal{B}_h is the linearized map \delta X_{\partial A} \to \phi_{\gamma_A}, taking a boundary deformation into the corresponding normal deformation of the extremal surface. Equivalently, the on-shell second variation with respect to boundary data is a boundary Dirichlet-to-Neumann/shape-Hessian operator derived from the bulk Jacobi problem.

The correct residual is therefore:

\boxed{
\Delta_2 = \left\| D^2 S - \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h \right\|
}

where D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h = \mathcal{B}_h^{*} J_h \mathcal{B}_h under the appropriate boundary conditions.

8.3 Response Residual (Corrected \eta Normalization)

CORRECTION. Since S = \eta \mathfrak{R}_h, we have D^2 S = \eta D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h. If the response operator is the inverse Hessian, \chi_S = (D^2 S)^{-1}, then:

\boxed{
\chi_S = \frac{1}{\eta} (D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h)^{-1}
}

So the response residual should be:

\boxed{
\Delta_{\text{resp}} = \left\| \chi_S - \frac{1}{\eta_{\mathcal{Q}}} \left( D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h \right)^{-1} \right\|
}

with a precise definition of \chi_S as the inverse of the entropic Hessian on the orthogonal complement of zero modes and with specified boundary conditions.

8.4 Interpretation

This is one of the strongest mathematical formulations of the program:

\boxed{\text{Entropic Hessian} \longleftrightarrow \text{Geometric Jacobi response}}

The entropic Hessian must be compatible with the Jacobi operator of the minimal surface. This is a testable operatorial structure, not merely a numerical correlation.

