Chapter 9 — The QFIM–Area Integrability Conjecture

9.1 Statement

QFIM–Area Integrability Conjecture.

Let \mathcal{Q} = (V, E, \rho, \mathcal{D}) be a local quantum system with finite correlation length \xi, and let g_Q denote a quantum-information metric obtained from a specified QFI construction. Assume that, after coarse-graining at a scale L \gg \xi, there exists a smooth non-degenerate spatial metric h_{ij}(x) = \Phi_{L*} g_Q on an emergent manifold M.

For a sufficiently rich class \mathcal{C} of boundary regions A, define \mathfrak{R}_h[A] = \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A[h]) where \gamma_A[h] is an h-minimal codimension-one surface satisfying \partial \gamma_A = \partial A and H_h[\gamma_A] = 0. Then an entanglement entropy functional S: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R} is geometrically integrable with respect to h if there exists a coefficient \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} > 0 such that:

S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \mathfrak{R}_h[A] + o(\mathfrak{R}_h[A])

in the continuum limit.

9.2 The Conjecture Is Not Universal

The conjecture is not that every quantum Fisher metric is geometrically integrable. Rather, it predicts that geometric integrability occurs only for quantum systems satisfying a specific set of locality, regularity, coarse-graining, and entropic consistency conditions.

9.3 Two Scaling Regimes

T2-G (gapped/finite-\xi): L/\xi \to \infty. T2-C (critical scaling): \xi = \infty, with a scaling variable such as L/a \to \infty and explicit universal/subleading terms. For a 1D CFT, S(\ell) = (c/3)\log(\ell/a) + s_0 + \cdots. M2 should not be described as merely "T2 with \xi \to \infty." It is a critical extension of T2.

9.4 The Central Mathematical Problem

Characterize the image \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R}) and determine when S \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R}_{g_Q}).

