Appendix B — The Hessian–Jacobi Correspondence

B.1 The Forward Functional and Its Variations

DEFINITION B.1 (Minimal-Surface Functional). Let \mathcal{M}_{\text{adm}} be the space of admissible Riemannian metrics on a manifold M of dimension d. Let \mathcal{C} be a sufficiently rich class of boundary regions A \subset \partial M. Define the forward functional:

\mathfrak{R}: \mathcal{M}_{\text{adm}}/\operatorname{Diff}(M) \rightarrow \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{C})

by

\mathfrak{R}[h](A) = \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A[h])

where \gamma_A[h] is the h-minimal codimension-one surface satisfying \partial \gamma_A = \partial A and H_h[\gamma_A] = 0.

B.2 First Variation

PROPOSITION B.2 (First Variation Compatibility). If S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \mathfrak{R}_h[A], then a variation of region A must produce the corresponding geometric variation:

\delta S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \delta \mathfrak{R}_h[A]

For an on-shell minimal surface whose boundary is being varied, the relevant variation reduces to a boundary term:

\delta \mathfrak{R}_h[A] = \int_{\partial \gamma_A} \Pi_i \, \delta X^i

up to the precise convention for the canonical momentum \Pi_i and boundary measure. Then:

\delta S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \, \delta \mathfrak{R}_h[A]

B.3 Second Variation and the Domain Mismatch

PROBLEM. The Jacobi operator J_h acts on normal deformations of the bulk minimal surface \gamma_A, while the entropic Hessian D^2 S acts on variations of the boundary region A. These are different vector spaces.

RESOLUTION. Introduce the linearized map:

\mathcal{B}_h: \text{boundary deformations of } A \to \text{normal deformations of } \gamma_A

taking a boundary deformation \delta X_{\partial A} to the corresponding normal deformation \phi_{\gamma_A} of the extremal surface. Then the correct operatorial statement is:

D^2 S_A[X, Y] = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \int_{\gamma_A} (\mathcal{B}_h X) \, J_h \, (\mathcal{B}_h Y) \, d\mu_\gamma

Equivalently:

\boxed{
\mathcal{H}_S = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \, \mathcal{B}_h^{\,*} J_h \mathcal{B}_h
}

where \mathcal{H}_S is the boundary shape Hessian of the minimized area functional.

B.4 Response Residual and \eta Normalization

Since S = \eta \mathfrak{R}_h, we have D^2 S = \eta D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h. If the response operator is the inverse Hessian, \chi_S = (D^2 S)^{-1}, then:

\chi_S = \frac{1}{\eta} (D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h)^{-1}

The response residual is therefore:

\boxed{
\Delta_{\text{resp}} = \left\| \chi_S - \frac{1}{\eta_{\mathcal{Q}}} \left( D^2 \mathfrak{R}_h \right)^{-1} \right\|
}

with a precise definition of \chi_S as the inverse of the entropic Hessian on the orthogonal complement of zero modes and with specified boundary conditions.

B.5 The Boundary Dirichlet-to-Neumann Formulation

The on-shell second variation with respect to boundary data is a boundary Dirichlet-to-Neumann/shape-Hessian operator derived from the bulk Jacobi problem. Specifically, the bulk Jacobi equation J_h \phi = 0 with Dirichlet boundary conditions \phi|_{\partial \gamma} = \delta X_{\partial A} defines a Dirichlet-to-Neumann map:

\Lambda_h: \delta X_{\partial A} \mapsto \partial_n \phi|_{\partial \gamma}

The boundary shape Hessian is then \mathcal{H}_{\mathfrak{R}, h} = \Lambda_h \circ \mathcal{B}_h. This formulation makes precise the sense in which the entropic Hessian corresponds to the geometric Jacobi response.

