Appendix C — Entropy Inequalities and Geometric Realizability

C.1 Holographic Entropy Inequalities

The holographic entropy inequalities (HEIs) are linear inequalities on the entanglement entropies of a collection of regions that are satisfied by any collection constructed from Ryu–Takayanagi surfaces in a classical geometry. The HEIs form the facets of the holographic entropy cone.

C.2 Strong Subadditivity

For any three regions A, B, C:

S_{AB} + S_{BC} \geq S_B + S_{ABC}

Strong subadditivity is satisfied by all quantum states and is therefore a necessary but not sufficient condition for holographic realizability.

C.3 Monogamy of Mutual Information

For three regions A, B, C:

S_{AB} + S_{BC} + S_{AC} \geq S_A + S_B + S_C + S_{ABC}

MMI does not hold for arbitrary quantum states but is satisfied by holographic states whose entropies are computed from RT surfaces in a classical geometry. It therefore provides a filter: if a state violates MMI, its entropy vector cannot be realized by RT surfaces in any classical geometry.

C.4 Geometric Realizability

THEOREM. Any collection of entropies constructed from RT surfaces in a classical geometry satisfies the HEIs.

CONJECTURE. Conversely, every entropy vector satisfying the HEIs is geometrically realizable. This is open for N \geq 6.

NUMERICAL EVIDENCE. The 2026 reinforcement learning study of He, Lee, and Ooguri analyzed the 6 "mystery" extreme rays of the N = 6 subadditivity cone that satisfy all known holographic entropy inequalities yet lacked graph realizations. They found realizations for 3 of the 6 rays, proving they are genuine extreme rays, while providing evidence that the remaining 3 are not realizable, implying unknown holographic inequalities exist for N = 6.

RIGOROUS FORMULATION:

\boxed{
\text{Satisfaction of the known HEIs is not known to be sufficient for holographic realizability.}
}

The 2026 reinforcement-learning study provides computational evidence concerning the remaining N = 6 candidate rays; this does not constitute a proof of non-realizability.

