For a sufficiently rich class \mathcal{C} of boundary regions A, define

\mathfrak{R}_h[A] = \operatorname{Area}_h(\gamma_A[h]),

where \gamma_A[h] is an h-minimal codimension-one surface satisfying

\partial \gamma_A = \partial A, \qquad H_h[\gamma_A] = 0.

Then an entanglement entropy functional S: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R} is geometrically integrable with respect to h if there exists a coefficient \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} > 0 such that

S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \mathfrak{R}_h[A] + o(\mathfrak{R}_h[A])

in the continuum limit.

A necessary set of compatibility conditions includes:

\frac{\delta S_A}{\delta X} = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \frac{\delta \mathfrak{R}_h[A]}{\delta X},

D^2 S_A = \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \, \mathcal{B}_h^{*} J_h \mathcal{B}_h,

together with:

1. consistency of the same metric h across all A \in \mathcal{C};
2. regularity and non-degeneracy of h;
3. compatibility of the entropic Hessian with the Jacobi operator of \gamma_A;
4. satisfaction of the necessary entropy inequalities implied by the geometric construction;
5. stability of the reconstruction under coarse-graining;
6. convergence of the residual

\Delta_{\text{geom}} = \frac{S_A - \eta_{\mathcal{Q}} \mathfrak{R}_h[A]}{S_A} \to 0.

The conjecture is not that every quantum Fisher metric is geometrically integrable. Rather, it predicts that geometric integrability occurs only for quantum systems satisfying a specific set of locality, regularity, coarse-graining, and entropic consistency conditions.

The central mathematical problem is therefore to characterize

\boxed{\operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R})}

and determine when

S \in \operatorname{Im}(\mathfrak{R}_{g_Q}).

