Appendix F — The Two Pipelines

F.1 Pipeline A (Information \to Geometry)

\rho \to g_{\mu\nu}^{Q} \to R_{\mu\nu} \to G_{\mu\nu}^{(A)}

This is the primary route. The QFIM g_{\mu\nu}^{Q} is computed independently from the state \rho, either directly from the SLD definition for the specified state family, or via the Williamson decomposition for Gaussian states. The Ricci tensor and Einstein tensor are then computed from the reconstructed metric.

F.2 Pipeline B (Information \to Thermodynamic Gravity)

\rho \to S_A \to \delta S_A \to \delta \langle K_A \rangle \to G_{\mu\nu}^{(B)}

This route uses the entanglement entropy S_A and the first law of entanglement \delta S_A = \delta \langle K_A \rangle to derive the linearized Einstein equations, following Faulkner et al. (2014).

F.3 Consistency Condition

\boxed{G_{\mu\nu}^{(A)} \approx G_{\mu\nu}^{(B)}}

in the continuum limit. This condition tests whether the QFIM-derived geometry and the entanglement-derived thermodynamics agree in the gravitational limit.

F.4 Route B (Distinguishability Graph)

As an alternative to Pipeline A, one may construct a distinguishability graph:

\rho_i, \rho_j \to D_Q(\rho_i,\rho_j) \to d_Q(i,j) \to h_{ij}

where D_Q(\rho_i,\rho_j) is a quantum distinguishability measure. Mutual information I(i:j) becomes a diagnostic for locality and correlation structure, not an unexplained intermediate tensor.

